      "attributes": [
        {
          "doc_text": " Provides the pointer metadata type of any pointed-to type.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            265,
            327
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            328,
            331
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " # Pointer metadata",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            332,
            354
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            355,
            358
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Raw pointer types and reference types in Rust can be thought of as made of two parts:",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            359,
            448
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " a data pointer that contains the memory address of the value, and some metadata.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            449,
            533
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            534,
            537
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " For statically-sized types (that implement the `Sized` traits)",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            538,
            604
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " as well as for `extern` types,",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            605,
            639
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " pointers are said to be “thin”: metadata is zero-sized and its type is `()`.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            640,
            724
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            725,
            728
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Pointers to [dynamically-sized types][dst] are said to be “wide” or “fat”,",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            729,
            815
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " they have non-zero-sized metadata:",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            816,
            854
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            855,
            858
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " * For structs whose last field is a DST, metadata is the metadata for the last field",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            859,
            947
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " * For the `str` type, metadata is the length in bytes as `usize`",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            948,
            1016
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " * For slice types like `[T]`, metadata is the length in items as `usize`",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1017,
            1093
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " * For trait objects like `dyn SomeTrait`, metadata is [`DynMetadata<Self>`][DynMetadata]",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1094,
            1186
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "   (e.g. `DynMetadata<dyn SomeTrait>`)",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1187,
            1228
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1229,
            1232
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " In the future, the Rust language may gain new kinds of types",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1233,
            1297
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " that have different pointer metadata.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1298,
            1339
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1340,
            1343
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " [dst]: https://doc.rust-lang.org/nomicon/exotic-sizes.html#dynamically-sized-types-dsts",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1344,
            1435
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1436,
            1439
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1440,
            1443
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " # The `Pointee` trait",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1444,
            1469
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1470,
            1473
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " The point of this trait is its `Metadata` associated type,",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1474,
            1536
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " which is `()` or `usize` or `DynMetadata<_>` as described above.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1537,
            1605
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " It is automatically implemented for every type.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1606,
            1657
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " It can be assumed to be implemented in a generic context, even without a corresponding bound.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1658,
            1755
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1756,
            1759
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1760,
            1763
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " # Usage",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1764,
            1775
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1776,
            1779
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Raw pointers can be decomposed into the data pointer and metadata components",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1780,
            1860
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " with their [`to_raw_parts`] method.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1861,
            1900
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1901,
            1904
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Alternatively, metadata alone can be extracted with the [`metadata`] function.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1905,
            1987
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " A reference can be passed to [`metadata`] and implicitly coerced.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1988,
            2057
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2058,
            2061
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " A (possibly-wide) pointer can be put back together from its data pointer and metadata",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            2062,
            2151
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " with [`from_raw_parts`] or [`from_raw_parts_mut`].",
